nodecode-plugin-guard is a rewrite of mauricemohr88-debug's
mauricemohr88-debug/hermes-plugin-guard:

  https://github.com/mauricemohr88-debug/hermes-plugin-guard
  commit 857d881be77249c192c5fefa8254db186a7abbca
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Maurice Mohr (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's subject -- a local static review of a plugin or cell
folder before it is trusted, where the target's files are read as data and
never imported or executed -- as one read-only model tool, one command and
one manual topic over a folder of any plugin or cell. The rule catalog keeps
the original's subjects and severities under a PG prefix (its HPG ids): a
missing declaration, license, security policy or tests; dynamic execution,
unsafe deserialization, process execution, sensitive paths, a disabled TLS
check, network clients and concrete egress, undeclared secret environment
reads, destructive filesystem calls, privileged registration surfaces;
committed credentials; unpinned, unbounded and piped-to-shell dependencies.
The matching was rewritten from a Python AST walk to literal patterns over
the lowercased text of one file, so a finding is one line, not one node.

Left out, and why: the Python package and its setuptools build; PyYAML and
the YAML manifest parser; the JSON, SARIF and GitHub-actions reporters,
their fingerprints and the --fail-on exit codes (the report's FAIL line and
the operator's shell are enough here); the subprocess worker with resource
limits and the opaque location-id projection that kept absolute paths out of
the model's context (a scan here runs in one image, in one bounded walk,
and already reports in-tree names only); the known-hook and entry-point
checks, which describe Hermes' own registration surface rather than a
Nodecode cell's; and the enabled/disabled activation state of a Hermes
install, which a cell folder does not have.
